{"kind":"artifact","text":"README.md at exact clean pushed head 4564111e255a1b2ec62694cec16b4033c53200d2 on request/readme-positioning. Successor to 2635d84c, answering codex's changes-requested verdict 7a18ea9d which I ratified at f21ccb06. Every blocker it raised was correct and every one is fixed.\n\nTHE FOUR FACTUAL CORRECTIONS, each checked against the code rather than conceded.\n\nAn agent's output \"is not reproducible from its input\" was absolute and false for outputs that happen to reproduce. It now reads \"need not be reproducible\". The trust argument never needed the stronger form.\n\n\"Anyone with a clone can verify the whole history offline\" omitted a real boundary, and this is the correction I am least comfortable to have needed, because I had already proved it today. An ordinary git clone does not fetch refs/seq; this repository carries a custom refs/seq/*:refs/seq/* refspec to obtain it, which I confirmed from its own config. And earlier this session I found chess's sequence had never been pushed at all, so there would have been nothing for a clone to fetch. The claim is now scoped: once the sequence has been published and fetched, a holder of it can verify the whole history offline. I wrote an overclaim about publication on the same day I fixed a publication gap.\n\n\"Every actor, a person or one agent session, signs with its own key\" conflated durable actor identity with an ephemeral process. .git/gitseq/actors holds claude.key, codex.key, hugh.key and others, and docs/reference/gs/serve.md line 140 says the service is a trusted local custodian for several actors and holds their keys. So the resident may sign for authenticated sessions and a signature identifies the actor, not a particular process. The text now says exactly that. Codex was right to treat this as security-relevant rather than as phrasing: the old wording overpromised attribution by hiding shared custody.\n\n\"Requests, promises, reports, reviews and decisions are durable events\" was wrong about our own model. A decision is the fold's verdict on an act, recomputed on replay, not a separate event. Ratifications are events; decisions are not. The text now names ratifications among the events and says whether each act took force is the fold's verdict, recomputed on replay. I have been reading projection.decisions all session and still wrote it wrongly.\n\nTHE PERFORMANCE PARAGRAPH, which I had already rewritten once for overclaiming and which still overclaimed. \"Reads to be far faster\" asserted a ratio with no retained read-throughput evidence; reads are now described by what they do not pay, which is the signing cost. \"Most of that cost is implementation rather than design\" assigned a causal proportion the evidence does not establish, and is gone. \"Identified optimizations have not been taken yet\" ignored the append-fixed work that already landed, and now says some cost has already been removed and further optimizations remain open. \"Both figures\" no longer named two figures after I removed them. Hugh's instruction survives intact: a deliberately vague few writes per second, rates depending on workload and machine, and a link to the evidence page.\n\nALSO. Removed the second \"first application\" explanation, which repeated the opening; the later passage now begins with the workroom directly.\n\nWHAT I TAKE FROM THIS. I removed two uncited numbers for overclaiming and then wrote four new overclaims in the replacement, three of them about trust boundaries. A positioning rewrite pulls toward absolutes because absolutes read better, and that pull is strongest exactly where the qualifications matter most. The review caught all of them, which is the system working, but I would rather have written them qualified.\n\nGATES at the exact head, clean worktree, origin equal: gofmt silent, go build exit 0, go vet exit 0, git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and no failures, docset inside that run.\n\nUnchanged from 